Original commit body from 9cbd12752957f852f6c1160e07937eb7ea2f7091:

INV-hahak. `wildcard_imports[0]` was applied unconditionally to every capitalised
receiver not explicitly imported, so under `import java.io.*;` the call
`System.currentTimeMillis()` landed as `java.io.System` and matched no row.
`_extract_wildcard_imports`' own docstring already promised "the first wildcard
whose package the receiver could plausibly belong to"; the resolver never
implemented it, and nothing failed loudly -- the file parses and the edge is
still emitted, so the loss surfaces as a catalogue miss, not a resolution error.

THE DOMINANT CASE IS NOT A HEURISTIC. JLS 7.3 makes java.lang.* implicitly
imported in every compilation unit, so System, String, Integer, Math and Thread
are in scope having never been written -- and a file carrying ANY wildcard
mis-attributed all of them. The module slot now carries the disjunction the
situation actually is: every wildcard package plus java.lang, reusing the
comma-joined contract cpp has used since INV-funuf.

THE UNQUALIFIED CLASS NAME IS DELIBERATELY NOT A CANDIDATE. Offering bare `Files`
would match any row whose module ends in `.Files`, so a project's own `Files`
class would reach java.nio.file.Files -- the INV-dijor false-positive shape
imported into a recall fix. Java ships zero function-kind rows (139 method, 3
attribute), so INV-nizom's multi-candidate `kind != function` filter discards
nothing here.

SECOND HALF: a FULLY-QUALIFIED call site was overridden.
`java.nio.file.Files.writeString(...)` under `import java.io.*;` was
re-attributed to java.io.Files, because the receiver arrives as a field_access
chain and only its last component survived. A chain whose leading components are
lowercase and whose last is capitalised is now read as a fully-qualified type and
used verbatim. That orthographic inference is java's own package/type convention
applied INSIDE the java analyzer -- the distinction the module-key audit drew,
since the same inference in io_boundary._module_matches runs language-agnostically
and is degenerate in haskell (100% capitalised modules), swift (97%), objc (95%)
and elixir (52%).

MEASURED on jedis + spring-petclinic, both arms cold in their own
XDG_CACHE_HOME: 36 boundaries added, 0 removed. System.currentTimeMillis x30 and
nanoTime x4 (host_info_read), System.getProperty x2 (env_read -- a taint SOURCE).
All read back against source: MultiDbConnectionProvider.java:567, and
TlsUtil.java:35 in a file carrying THREE wildcards (javax.net.ssl.*, java.io.*,
java.security.*), where the old code picked javax.net.ssl.System and matched
nothing. Provably-wrong population sized at 170 external refs in jedis alone.

THE FILED REPRO WAS NON-COMPILING JAVA and the real shape is different: under a
single wildcard the class usually DOES live in that package, so the item's
1622-file figure is not the affected set. The genuinely broken populations are
java.lang classes (every file with any wildcard), files with two or more
competing wildcards (464), and fully-qualified call sites. System.in/System.out
were checked and are unaffected -- they travel as module_attr_ref with module
slot `System` and never enter this branch.

